It meets the $x$-axis at exactly one point $x=r$, so there are only four possible answers (take $a>0$ first):
{{math:1}}
· “$>0$” — true for every real number except the single point $x=r$;
· “$\ge 0$” — true for all real numbers (the point $x=r$ counts);
· “$<0$” — no solution;
· “$\le 0$” — the single solution $x=r$.

If the coefficient of $x^{2}$ is negative, multiply both sides by $-1$ (reversing the inequality sign) and you are back to the four cases above.
Example: $x^{2}-6x+9=(x-3)^{2}${{math:2}}

