When objects are divided into groups, ask first: are the groups labelled (group 1, group 2, ...)?
- Labelled, or in a stated order: choose group 1 first, then group 2, and multiply the counts;
- Not labelled: groups of the same size are counted $k!$ times ($k$ = number of groups of that size), so divide by $k!$.
The relation is: {{math:0}}
Example: 6 people divided into 2 groups of 3.
- Labelled (one team to a maths contest, one to a science contest): $C^6_3 \times C^3_3 = 20$ ways;
- Unlabelled (two teams for a street-ball game): swapping the two 3-person teams is the same match, so $\frac{C^6_3 \times C^3_3}{2!} = 10$ ways.
One level further: 10 people divided into groups of 4, 4 and 2 — with no labels only $2!$ is cancelled (only the two 4-person groups duplicate each other), not $3!$, giving $\frac{C^{10}_4 C^6_4 C^2_2}{2!} = 1575$ ways.
